  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is defined by\nstrong ridge of high pressure centered over west-central Texas \nextending as far west as ERN Arizona. The latest GOES precipitable\nwater imagery indicated generally 1.40-1.60 inches of PWAT \nacross SEAZ with drier values AOA 1.25 inches in the extreme \ncorner of the state. The imagery also indicated sub 1 inch PWATs\nin New Mexico and Chihuahua Mexico that will move into the CWA \nthis afternoon. Given the combination of warming temps aloft \nand the general drying, we will struggle to see anything more \nthan isolated convection across our neck of the woods today. The\nbig change with the forecast will occur the next few days as \nthe strong upper-high center moves WSW into SRN New Mexico by \n24/00Z this afternoon then into ERN Arizona by 25/00Z Friday \nafternoon. We will see afternoon temperatures jump rapidly today\nwith another increase Friday and an ever so slight increase \nSaturday as the upper high center gets setup near the Four \nCorners.\n\nAs stated from the previous forecast, the hottest days expected\nare Friday and Saturday. Temperatures will range from 105 to \n112 degrees in the Tucson Metro, Western Deserts, and Upper Gila\nRiver Valley. Elsewhere temperatures will range from 100 to 105\ndegrees. This increase in temperatures in combination with the \nwarm low temperatures of 80 to 85 degrees have necessitated an \nExtreme Heat Warning for Friday and Saturday when temperatures \nwill be 6 to 10 degrees above normal. Major HeatRisk is expected\nfor the valleys, so be aware of the signs for heat illness/stroke,\nstay hydrated, stay indoors with air conditioning when possible,\nand consider moving plans indoors during the hottest time of \nday.\n\nOtherwise, with the upper-high in a more favorable location \nnear the Four Corners Saturday, this increases the likelihood \nof thunderstorms moving off the Mogollon Rim into Tucson in the\nlate afternoon. The combination of very strong surface heating,\ndrier air aloft and modest mid-level flow will increase the \npotential for stronger, more organized clustering of showers and\nthunderstorms Saturday. An additional surge of deep moisture is\nthen expected to move into Southeast Arizona late this weekend \nas an easterly wave associated with the remains of Tropical \nStorm Bertha moves west into the southern Gulf of California \ninitiating a gulf surge.",
